
# `.beads/issues.jsonl` is a whole-file dump of a database every concurrent
# session writes to. `-merge` is git's own attribute, not a driver: on a conflict
# git leaves the file at OUR bytes and marks the path conflicted, instead of
# line-merging two dumps and writing `<<<<<<<` markers INTO the JSON.
# Hand-resolving those markers is how a session silently drops its siblings'
# beads. Resolve by taking origin's copy whole, never by editing the file:
#
#     git checkout --theirs -- .beads/issues.jsonl && git add .beads/issues.jsonl
#     git commit          # finish the merge
#     br-export           # reconciles origin's view into the DB, then writes
#
# Do NOT name a merge driver here. This line read `merge=beads` until 2026-09-05.
# The driver was `bd merge %A %O %A %B`, configured only in untracked
# .git/config; `bd` was retired for `br`, `br` has no `merge` subcommand, and
# nothing tracked ever wrote that config. So `git merge` either printed
# `bd: command not found` and fell through to the very line merge this line is
# supposed to prevent, or — in a checkout with no local config at all — died with
# `fatal: custom merge driver beads lacks command line` and refused to merge.
# Measured both ways in bead .agent-config-4vtpw.
.beads/issues.jsonl -merge
